  "notes": [
   "The parts are the same as in step 1; nothing was added or dropped.",
   "The circuit image shows a Pico-style GP-labelled board, so the pi-pico type is used. GPIO14, GPIO15 and GPIO4 map to ports GP14, GP15 and GP4. The GND pin next to GP4 is given as GND.1.",
   "Breadboard naming follows the catalogue: rows a-e are bank 't' and rows f-j are bank 'b'. The image has rows j-f on top and e-a below the centre ridge. The blue rail at the bottom is 'bn'. Rail hole numbers are counted in groups of 5 from the left: the left button's black wire is at bn.1, the resistor at bn.10, the GND wire at bn.19 and the right button's black wire at bn.25.",
   "The left button is in columns 1 and 3 and the right button in columns 28 and 30. Per the text, each button's legs are in rows h and j, so the two legs in one column are the same side (1.l/1.r in the first column, 2.l/2.r in the second). The wires are in row f of those columns.",
   "The LED's long leg (anode) is in column 16 and its short leg (cathode) in column 15, both on row e as in the image. The text says D, but the image shows e, so the image is followed. The resistor is in column 15, row a, in the LED's cathode strip. Its other leg goes to the blue rail (GND), so the resistor is on the cathode side. GPIO4 goes to column 16, row a, the anode strip.",
   "For the male-to-female jumpers, port 'a' is the male pin end into the breadboard and port 'b' is the female socket end on the Pico header. The two black pin-to-pin wires are plain jumper-wire types."
  ]
